==English==

===Pronunciation===
* {{audio|en|En-au-on the spot.ogg|Audio (AU)}}

===Prepositional phrase===
{{en-prep phrase}}

# {{lb|en|idiomatic}} At that very moment.
#: {{syn|en|right away|then and there}}
#: {{ux|en|He liked the house, so he made an offer '''on the spot'''.}}
# {{lb|en|idiomatic}} Having to [[answer]] or [[decide]] without [[warning]] or [[preparation]].
#: {{ux|en|All the sudden questions put him '''on the spot''' and he had to think quickly.}}
# {{lb|en|idiomatic}} In a particular place.
#: {{syn|en|in place|on location|on site|on the premises|in the room}}
#: {{ux|en|This report comes from our man '''on the spot''' in Gaza.}}
#: {{ux|en|We exercised by jumping up and down '''on the spot'''.}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1928|author=w:Dorothy L. Sayers|chapter=The Abominable History of the Man with Copper Fingers|title=w:Lord Peter Views the Body
|passage=I had accepted an engagement to go out with Mystofilms Ltd. in ''Fake of Dead Man's Bush''—the dwarf-men picture, you know, taken '''on the spot''' among the Australian bushmen.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|year=1945|month=September and October|author=C. Hamilton Ellis|title=Royal Trains—V|journal=Railway Magazine|pages=251–252|text={{...}} on October 29, 1888, the Russian imperial train was derailed at Borki by defective track, and twenty-one persons were killed. Although these did not include the Emperor Alexander III, who escaped with a bruising, a footman serving coffee to him at the critical moment, and his dog, which was lying on the floor beside him, were both killed '''on the spot'''.}}

